<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>While I feel that &quot;The Mother Nature is rising against us&quot; idea is a little bit overused, it is fine and it is executed with a touch of bodily horror.</p>
<p>But the revelation in the end that an Ethics Comittee member is behind an outbreak of an SCP feels very wierd. I rememer a similar idea used for the Shy Guy (096), but I feel it was better there. Let me elaborate:</p>
<p>A researcher, staging a containment breach to prove the dangers of a scip to O5-council - that is compelling, it serves a narrative purpose and explores something about the Foundation and the idea of a termination of and SCP.</p>
<p>In this article an EC-member stages a containment breach - but for what? The SCP was contained, it doesn't really require termination so what was the point? To show the reader that Dr. Calloway is a compulsive, volatile individual? To show that EC is corruptable or maybe even inhuman? While the latter is an interesting idea to explore, it gets really mudded by the added touch of rebelling nature. And the resulting dialog feels inconsistent and misplaced</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I feel sorry for it, but as it stands, I feel compeled to downvote.</p>

